    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>If your Houston-area home or building was damaged in a storm, the claim usually turns on causation: did wind (and rain coming through a storm-created opening) do the damage, or did rising floodwater? That one finding decides which policy pays, which deductible applies, and how much you recover. We inspect the property, document the cause, write a line-by-line Xactimate estimate, and negotiate with the carrier on your behalf.</p>
<p>We're based in Belton and travel to the Houston area to inspect property in person. Before a visit, we can review your policy and the carrier's estimate by phone or text at no cost.</p>

<h2>Why Houston claims get split between policies</h2>
<p>Houston has been through Hurricane Ike (2008), Hurricane Harvey (2017) and Hurricane Beryl (2024). Each storm damaged property in a different way. Ike pushed storm surge onto Galveston and the bay communities. Harvey stalled and dropped days of rain, so most of its damage was flooding. Beryl was a wind event for much of the metro, with trees on roofs and long power outages.</p>
<p>When wind and water both hit a building, the carriers each look for reasons the damage belongs to someone else. A standard homeowners policy excludes flood. A flood policy (NFIP or private) covers rising water but not wind. Many homeowners policies also contain wording that excludes flood damage even when another cause contributed at the same time, so the evidence of what happened first, and where, matters.</p>
<div class="compare">
<div><h3>Points toward wind coverage</h3><ul>
<li>Missing shingles, lifted flashing or a punctured roof deck</li>
<li>Ceiling and upper-wall staining below a roof or window opening</li>
<li>Broken windows or doors that let rain in</li>
<li>Damage above the high-water line inside the house</li>
</ul></div>
<div><h3>Points toward flood coverage</h3><ul>
<li>A consistent waterline on walls throughout the floor</li>
<li>Silt or debris left by water that came in at ground level</li>
<li>Water entering under doors and through weep holes</li>
<li>Damage limited to flooring, base cabinets and lower drywall</li>
</ul></div>
</div>
<p>Most Houston homes are slab-on-grade, so even a few inches of water can ruin flooring, baseboards, lower cabinets and the bottom of every interior wall. If the roof was also damaged, the two losses need to be separated room by room. We photograph the waterline, the roof openings and the interior staining before anything is torn out, so the record survives demolition.</p>

<h2>TWIA covers only part of the region</h2>
<p>The Texas Windstorm Insurance Association writes wind and hail coverage only in the 14 first-tier coastal counties and in parts of Harris County east of Highway 146. Around Houston, that means Galveston, Brazoria and Chambers counties and that slice of eastern Harris County. If your property is in Katy, Cypress, The Woodlands or most of Houston itself, your wind coverage is in your regular homeowners or commercial policy, not TWIA.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Where the property is</th><th>Wind and hail</th><th>Flood</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Galveston, Brazoria or Chambers County</td><td>TWIA or a private carrier</td><td>Separate NFIP or private flood policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harris County east of Highway 146</td><td>TWIA or a private carrier</td><td>Separate NFIP or private flood policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rest of Harris, Fort Bend and Montgomery counties</td><td>Homeowners or commercial policy</td><td>Separate NFIP or private flood policy</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<div class="callout callout--law"><p><strong>Texas law:</strong> TWIA claims must be filed within one year of the date of damage, and TWIA generally has 60 days to accept or deny after it receives the claim and the information it requested (Tex. Ins. Code §2210.573). Private policies set their own reporting terms, so read your policy's "Duties after loss" section.</p></div>

[[cta]]

<h2>Deductibles on a Houston wind claim</h2>
<p>Coastal and hurricane-exposed policies often carry a wind or named-storm deductible written as a percentage of dwelling coverage, commonly 1–2%. On a home insured for a high dwelling limit, that can mean the first several thousand dollars of wind damage is yours. A carrier estimate that lands just under the deductible is a common Houston outcome, and it's often because items were left off: drip edge, starter strip, ridge vents, interior paint to the nearest corner, or contents. The <a href="/tools/claim-settlement-calculator/">claim settlement calculator</a> shows how the deductible and depreciation change what you're paid.</p>

<h2>What to do after a Houston storm</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Photograph before you tear out.</strong> Shoot the waterline on every wall, the roof from the ground, ceiling stains, and every damaged item. Wet drywall must come out quickly to stop mold, but the record has to exist first.</li>
<li><strong>Report to every carrier that may owe.</strong> File with your homeowners or TWIA carrier for wind and with your flood carrier for rising water. Write down each claim number and the date you reported.</li>
<li><strong>Dry the structure and keep the receipts.</strong> Mitigation companies use moisture meters and drying logs. Keep their reports; they show what was wet and for how long.</li>
<li><strong>Tarp roof openings.</strong> Reasonable temporary repairs are part of your duty to protect the property, and the cost is usually part of the claim.</li>
<li><strong>Don't accept the first causation finding as final.</strong> If the adjuster calls everything flood, ask what evidence supports that and request the engineer's report if one was used.</li>
</ol>
<p>After a declared catastrophe, the insurer's Chapter 542 deadlines to accept, reject and pay are extended by 15 days. Our <a href="/tools/texas-claim-deadline-calculator/">Texas claim deadline calculator</a> works out the dates from your own claim timeline.</p>

<h2>Freeze and water claims between hurricanes</h2>
<p>Not every Houston claim is a hurricane. Winter Storm Uri in 2021 burst pipes in attics and exterior walls across the metro, and summer storms bring hail and wind to the northern and western suburbs. Water claims from a burst line are handled differently from flood: the question is whether the discharge was sudden and accidental, and whether the policy limits mold or long-term seepage. Our guide to <a href="/post/dealing-with-insurance-adjuster-after-water-damage/">dealing with the adjuster after water damage</a> covers what to say and what to document.</p>

<h2>How we work a Houston-area claim</h2>
<ul class="checklist">
<li>Review your homeowners, TWIA and flood declarations to see which policy should respond to which damage</li>
<li>Inspect in person with roof-level and drone photos and moisture readings</li>
<li>Write a room-by-room Xactimate estimate that separates wind and water damage</li>
<li>Attend re-inspections and respond to engineer reports</li>
<li>Advise on appraisal when the dispute is about the amount of loss</li>
</ul>
<p>Our fee is a percentage of what the insurer pays, capped by Texas law at 10%, with no upfront charge. See our <a href="/services/hurricane-windstorm-claims/">hurricane and windstorm claims</a> page for how we handle wind losses in more detail.</p>
